when voter turnout is depressed it tends  to help the candidate that's further to  the right and this applies between  Democrats and Republicans but also  within primaries with the more  conservative of the Democrats in a  primary the more conservative of the  Republicans in a primary and feeling  like there's no point to voting because  it's so non-competitive  would would only depress voter turnout  further as you're sort of pointing out  is there a number you could give us bill  like for example in order for Democrats  to win 50% of House seats nationally  they would need to get X percent of the  total number of votes that might  illuminate how skewed the situation is  yeah I don't have that exact number but  you're right it would take more than 50  because it would you know we do have a  situation again where you do have you do  have living patterns such that you you  can't you know even if you have compact  contiguous districts which is what is  required in most states and and and  certainly would would lead to more  greater communities of interest which is  what we're altima trying for but because  of voting patterns or living patterns  rather it's not a perfect system so you  do have to live you you do have to allow  for that I think in Virginia for  instance even though we are statewide  Democrats are appear to be at an  advantage if we had if we had that  perfect system where we removed  gerrymandering as a as a factor we would  probably still have Republican control  in our House of Delegates for instance  it would probably be fifty five out of a  hundred rather than 66 out of a hundred  though major difference no doubt about  it when you're talking about breaking  gridlock and passing bills let's talk a  little bit about other ways that this  could be done California passed  Proposition 11 which put issues of  redistricting into the hands of a  nonpartisan Commission back in 2008 is  that a good strategy what other  strategies exist and how can the average  voter get involved in this right many  many many states are looking at  independent redistricting commissions  and I think that's I think that's  important now they take many different  forms in California they have a system  where virtually anybody can can apply to  be on that commish  California has a strong as many Western  states do a very strong kind of voter  initiative kind of system and that  probably led since that's how  proposition 11 was passed that led to  them being the leader in this also  another thing they have out in  California that's worth mentioning that  would be very very difficult in a state  that doesn't have voter initiative is  they have their top to finish or  primaries where instead of having a  separate Republican and Democratic  primary you have one primary everybody  runs in that primary and the top two  finishers go on to meet in the general  election sometimes those top two  finishers are gonna both be Democrats  sometimes they're going to both be  Republicans but they're both going to  have to appeal to the middle or the  successful one is going to be the one  that tends to appeal to the middle so  that's what has had a big effect in  moderating out there in other states you  know in Virginia the the the the one  we're talking about the system we're  talking about right now would be where  you would have some a commission with a  couple of Democrats a couple of  Republicans but there would also be  three members that would be nonpartisan  state officials sort of like the  executive director of the Virginia State  Bar who's not considered to be a  Republican or a Democrat doesn't mean  they don't have opinions obviously but  they are not that they are used to  playing nonpartisan roles and the idea  also being that whatever whatever plan  they put forward that they come up with  and put forward has to have a  supermajority so it if we had that kind  of commission and both Republicans or  both Democrats opposed the plan that was  put forward it could not go forward they  would have to have it wouldn't have to  have at least bipartisan support so bill  or so you know a couple years out now  from the Census and then after that we  will have the redistricting at what  point and in what ways can individuals  who care about this issue get involved  be it with the census or whether the  with the actual districting process in  their state to have an impact here good  question or the thing  we're finding that of course is really  important is for people to care enough  to call their representatives and their  senators as well because the state  Senate even though they you know the  state Senate is is subject to this as  well they you know let them know that  you care they tend to realize that the  the issues that people are most likely  to call them about are ones that involve  their taxes or involve their children's  or their children or their schools they  don't think people care about  redistricting but as people come to  understand just the damaging effects  that are happening because of  redistricting in a gerrymandered way  people are getting involved they're  calling their representatives and they  need to do that so it becomes an issue  and and it really is a good government  issue  even in Virginia former governor and  senator George Allen a Republican who  was a victim of gerrymandering when he  was in the US House at the hands of a  Democratic state legislature he has  called it ridiculous and said reform is  needed so that's what people need to do  is let their representatives know it's  being attacked on a number of fronts  it's being attacked in the Legislature's  it's gerrymandering is being attacked  through in some cases voter initiative  when that's available in the courts we  have a we have significant cases that  are coming one that's coming up very  soon before the Supreme Court that could  have a a fact or an effect across the  board across the United States and how  these districts are drawn and in as a  result who gets elected the documentary  is jerry-rigged  turning democracy on its head we've been  speaking with the film's producer and  director Bill Oglesby who's also an  assistant professor at Virginia  Commonwealth University's Robertson  School of Media and culture bill  super-interesting important and an issue  we're going to be staying on so thank  you so much for talking to us about it I  appreciate you doing that David thank  you it's been good talking with you
 

